WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
THE PODCAST AVALANCHE:
Podcasts are taking over YouTube. Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:10.380
from financial influencers and gamers to fitness&nbsp;
models and vloggers all suddenly have a podcasts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.040
This is in stark contrast to everything the&nbsp;
media has been telling us. Our attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:23.280
span is shrinking. TikTok is beating YouTube.&nbsp;
Long form content is dying. YouTube shorts are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:30.480
taking over and on and on and on. The reality&nbsp;
is that this is completely false. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.780
companies are backing away from short form&nbsp;
content. Meta has eliminated their Reels creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.840
fund and TikTok is becoming more and more like&nbsp;
YouTube. Conversely, what seems to be doing really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:49.140
well is actually podcasts despite them breaking&nbsp;
basically every creator rule you can think of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:56.700
For one, they’re 1 to 2 hours long, if not longer,&nbsp;
so they’re by no means catering to our short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:02.940
attention spans. Also, there’s no fancy editing,&nbsp;
sound effects, music, graphics, or coherent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.280
storytelling that keeps people enticed. More&nbsp;
times than not, podcasts are just conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.040
that naturally jump from one topic to another.&nbsp;
Not to mention, there’s only one episode per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:20.400
week or maybe only one episode per month. Yet,&nbsp;
despite all these quote on quote shortfalls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.580
podcasts are performing better than ever. The&nbsp;
Iced Coffee Hour has nearly half a million subs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:33.360
the PBD Podcast has 700,000 subs, Lex Fridman&nbsp;
has nearly 3 million subs, and heck even Logan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:40.500
Paul’s podcast has 4.5 million subs. It seems&nbsp;
like podcasts are the new vlogging where every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.920
YouTuber has a podcast channel and they all rack&nbsp;
in millions of views. YouTube is of course trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.860
to capitlize on this trend by pushing podcasts as&nbsp;
much as possible. Just yesterday, YouTube rolled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:58.200
out podcast functionality on the music app. You&nbsp;
no longer have to be a premium member to listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.860
to podcasts in the background. But, speaking of&nbsp;
vlogging, that trend didn’t last for too long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:11.280
Eventually, most vloggers quit and it was only a&nbsp;
select few channels like Caisey Neistat who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.040
been able to survive in the genre over the long&nbsp;
term and even he took a massive break. So, why are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:23.520
podcasts taking over the platform and is this&nbsp;
just a short term fad or the future of YouTube.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.340
THE JOE ROGAN EFFECT:&nbsp;

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:36.420
Taking a look back, the explosion of podcasts&nbsp;
on YouTube can be traced back to one man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:42.480
Joe Rogan. Rogan basically invented the podcasting&nbsp;
niche here on YouTube having started his podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:48.960
the Joe Rogan Experience way back in 2009. Given&nbsp;
his background as a comedian and showhost on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.840
mainstream media, he was naturally pretty good at&nbsp;
having engaging and enticing conversations. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:01.200
show was almost like Good Mythical Morning which&nbsp;
was also blowing up at the time, but instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.780
focusing on production and entertainment value,&nbsp;
Rogan would focus on educational and intellectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:12.840
value. This isn’t to say that he instantly blew&nbsp;
up, but it gave him a differentiating factor which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:19.140
he leaned into over the years. He would interview&nbsp;
everyone from Elon Musk and Neil deGrasse Tyson to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:25.320
David Blaine and Bernie Sanders. Over time, this&nbsp;
gave him access to an extremely large audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:33.660
In fact, by the end of 2020, he had 10 million&nbsp;
subscribers and nearly 3 billion views. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:40.860
all of this would change in 2021. You see,&nbsp;
Spotify offered Rogan an exclusivity deal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:46.680
was worth as much as $200 million. Rogan would&nbsp;
of course take the deal and he would move his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:52.440
podcasting over to Spotify starting at end of&nbsp;
2020. Conventional wisdom would tell you that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:58.620
this is terrible news for YouTube. Spotify had&nbsp;
effectively poached the most popular podcaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:04.500
in the world. And with that who knows how many&nbsp;
loyal listeners they had converted to Spotify,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:12.180
but this is where things get dicey. Rogan is&nbsp;
obviously still extraordinarily popular pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:18.240
in an average of 11 million listeners per episode.&nbsp;
For perspective, the most popular mainstream media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:24.480
show only pulls in 3.24 million viewers per&nbsp;
episode. So, Rogan is obviously crushing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.000
but that doesn’t mean that YouTube is performing&nbsp;
poorly without him. In fact, Twitch has found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.280
that large creators leaving the platform is&nbsp;
surprisingly beneficial for them. Here’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.200
thing, when there’s a super large creator in&nbsp;
a given niche, they basically soak up all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:45.840
attention. This makes it extremely difficult for&nbsp;
smaller creators to gain traction as viewers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.300
simply satisfied with the larger creator. But,&nbsp;
when the larger creator switches platforms or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.860
takes a break, this allows for content from&nbsp;
smaller creators to be pushed more at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:01.860
time that viewers are looking to fill a void. This&nbsp;
creates the perfect storm for smaller creators to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:08.160
explode in popularity as was the case with Twitch.&nbsp;
Apparently, Ninja leaving Twitch allowed for 5 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.340
6 new streamers to take his place. And it seems&nbsp;
that this same phenomenon has played out on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:20.040
much bigger scale with podcasting on YouTube.&nbsp;
The best part of all of this for platforms is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:25.200
that the creator who left often tends to come&nbsp;
back. Rogan’s deal with Spotify, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:32.160
only lasts for 3.5 years. This means that the&nbsp;
deal will expire in early to mid 2024 which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.500
only a year away. If he returns to YouTube&nbsp;
at this point, I’m sure he’ll do great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:44.340
but he would no longer be the only podcasting&nbsp;
giant on the platform. He would simply be a giant.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.580
IRRESISTIBLE OPPORTUNITY:&nbsp;

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:58.560
So, that explains the audience side of things. Joe&nbsp;
Rogan had an extremely big podcasting presence on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.300
YouTube, and his departure left tens of millions&nbsp;
if not hundreds of millions of fans looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:08.820
for an alternative, allowing for up and coming&nbsp;
creators to blow up. But, what about the creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:14.160
side of things? If you take a look at the biggest&nbsp;
creators on the platform like Mr. Beast, Airrack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:20.340
and MrWhoseTheBoss, you’ll see that they’re all&nbsp;
taking the exact opposite approach. Every second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:27.180
of their videos is all about constant stimulation.&nbsp;
Text on screen, sound effects, constant cuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:33.420
super high energy, and tons of money. Clearly,&nbsp;
this is working out great for them and while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:39.180
this style will definitely evolve over time,&nbsp;
it’s most likely the future of YouTube. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:44.820
there’s a reason that movies are so popular.&nbsp;
People love high production value content. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.920
with that being said, podcasters won’t be&nbsp;
taking on these behemoths anytime soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:57.780
but what podcasting does offer is super high ROI&nbsp;
on a much smaller scale. Take the upfront work for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:03.780
example. Creating one of my videos takes about 20&nbsp;
hours of work between me and my editors. Creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:09.060
something on the level of MrWhoseTheBoss takes&nbsp;
hundreds of hours of work. And creating something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.940
the level of MrBeast takes thousands of hours&nbsp;
of work. But, at the end of the day, all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:21.480
effort only translates to a 10 to 20 minute long&nbsp;
video. Creating a podcast on the other hand only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:28.740
takes 3-5 hours of work. Worst case scenario, it&nbsp;
might take 10 hours of work, but at the end of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:36.420
you have hours of content. So, the ratio between&nbsp;
work input and content output is simply ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:42.120
and that’s just the first advantage. The second&nbsp;
advantage is that the demographics of podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:48.600
listeners are insane. Given the educational&nbsp;
nature of podcasts, it simply doesn’t appeal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:53.940
the general audience which largely limits how big&nbsp;
these channels can get. But, the people that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.900
does reach is insane. The people who are listening&nbsp;
to podcasts are generally high performers looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:04.140
to improve themselves, learn, and get to the&nbsp;
next level. In fact, 30% of podcast listeners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:10.680
make a quarter million per year. That right there&nbsp;
is a gold mine for advertisers leading to super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:16.560
high ad rates on podcasts. Not to mention, given&nbsp;
the length of podcasts, you can stuff in like 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:22.740
mid roll ads leading to even more income. But,&nbsp;
honestly the money and reach are the smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:28.500
advantages. The real advantage in my opinion&nbsp;
is the opportunity to network. As a podcaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.700
you’re constantly reaching out to leaders that&nbsp;
you would have otherwise never reached out to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:38.700
People like Elon Musk and Mark Zuckerberg, and you&nbsp;
get to meet and talk with these people for hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:44.760
So, really you’ve got low effort high&nbsp;
output evergreen content that appeals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.840
to a high value audience and allows you&nbsp;
to network with the most successful people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:54.060
in the world. No wonder every YouTuber is&nbsp;
trying to capitilize on this opportunity.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:09:03.840
UNSUSTAINABLE:
Not only are podcasts great for creators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:10.680
viewers, and advertisers, but they’re also great&nbsp;
for platforms. One of the biggest challenges for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.840
modern social media platforms is trying to&nbsp;
keep your attention. Attention is really the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.700
currency of the modern world. If you’re able to&nbsp;
garner attention, everything else will follow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:26.220
This is why social media platforms are constantly&nbsp;
tinkering with their algorithm and looking for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:31.620
perfect balance. It just so happens to be that&nbsp;
podcasts are extraordinarily easy to recommend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:38.100
as they pull in a stupid amount of watch time&nbsp;
given their length. Podcasts often boast 20, 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:44.100
40 minutes of average watch time. But, not only&nbsp;
do podcasts pull in a stupid amount of watch time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:50.160
but they also build habits and regular viewership.&nbsp;
Here’s the thing, people aren’t listening to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.800
podcasts on a friday evening when they’re trying&nbsp;
to unwind. Rather, podcasts usually make their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:01.260
way into people’s daily routines. We’re talking&nbsp;
about commutes to work, taking a walk, cooking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:07.440
folding the laundry, doing chores, etc. More times&nbsp;
than not, podcasts are the perfect tool to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:13.320
these mundane tasks more productive and it becomes&nbsp;
a part of the routine. So, platforms like YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:20.160
and Spotify don’t really have to push podcasts too&nbsp;
hard. When the time comes, people go and find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:26.400
podcasts they wanna listen to by themselves. This&nbsp;
makes podcasts likely the easiest way for these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:34.620
platforms to garner and retain attention, but it’s&nbsp;
not all good news. With everyone jumping onto the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:40.440
podcasting scene, there’s no question that this&nbsp;
area is going to become saturated if it isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:46.800
already. I mean, there’s only so many finance&nbsp;
podcasts, gaming podcasts, fitness podcasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:52.440
and food podcasts that people can listen to.&nbsp;
Also, something else to note is that much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:58.500
popularity of these podcasts can be attributed to&nbsp;
the mystery surrounding the guests. Take MrBeast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:03.780
for example. Before podcasts starting blowing&nbsp;
up YouTube, the intricacies of his business and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:09.660
operations were largely a mystery. But, now that&nbsp;
Jimmy has made his rounds on dozens of podcasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:15.060
much of the mystery surrounding his business&nbsp;
is now gone. People’s curiosities have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:20.700
satiated. A perfect example of this is Alex&nbsp;
Hormozi. In the early days, there was a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:26.640
of curiosity regarding who this lumberjack is&nbsp;
and how he made a $100 million. As such, his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:32.220
first appearance on the Iced Coffee Hour pulled in&nbsp;
nearly a million views. But, his second appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:37.380
on the show only garnered 300,000 views. And,&nbsp;
his latest appearance only garnered 200,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:42.600
views. This isn’t to say that these podcasts are&nbsp;
any less valuable, but simply that much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:48.960
mystery and interest surrounding Alex has faded&nbsp;
away. And as more and more podcasts interview the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:55.020
same people, it’s likely that Alex’s situation&nbsp;
will become a trend. Now of course, there will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.020 --> 00:12:00.180
always be new figures of interest, but there’s&nbsp;
no question that this list is not nearly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:06.900
exhaustive as it once used to be which raises the&nbsp;
question: how sustainable really is podcasting?

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:09.240
THE FATE OF PODCASTS:&nbsp;

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:20.100
None of this is to say that podcasting is going&nbsp;
to die into oblivion like Vine. But, there’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:24.960
question that the area is becoming oversaturated&nbsp;
and there’s only a limited amount of attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:30.660
and a limited number of guests. The early signs&nbsp;
of this are already starting to surface. Newer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:35.760
podcasts aren’t garnering nearly as much momentum,&nbsp;
popular podcasts are getting most of their views&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:41.520
and growth from short form content, and podcasts&nbsp;
are having to repeat guests more and more. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:46.860
I think it’s safe to say that the golden days&nbsp;
of podcasts are right now or are already behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:52.500
us. Again, this isn’t to say that podcasts&nbsp;
are going to disappear into oblivion. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:58.200
it’s more than likely that many of the newer&nbsp;
podcasters won’t end up making it and the legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:03.960
podcasts will stabilize at a much more modest view&nbsp;
count similar to vlogging. Vlogging isn’t dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:11.460
but it’s also not what it used to be back in 2015&nbsp;
and 2016. And that may very well be the fate of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:17.940
podcasts as well, but that’s just what I think.&nbsp;
